{"id":"qftx-scale-cocycles","title":"Scale cocycles, finite scheme changes and controlled RG equations","kind":"A","category":"quantum-field-theory-mathematics","library":"mathematics","companion":"qftx-scale-cocycles-examples","requires":["qftx-causal-renormalization","qftx-heavy-field-eft"],"items":[{"id":"def-qftx-scaling-cocycle","kind":"definition","domain":"mathematics","library":"mathematics","title":"Formal scaling and renormalization cocycle","statement":"Assume Countable Choice.\n\nLet $\\mathbb M=\\mathbb R^4$ have Minkowski metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. A scalar local polynomial functional is a finite sum\n$$F(\\phi)=c+\\sum_{j=1}^{N}\\int_{\\mathbb M} f_j(x)P_j(j^{s_j}\\phi(x))\\,d^4x,$$\nwhere $N$ is a finite nonnegative integer, $\\phi\\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb M;\\mathbb R)$, $c\\in\\mathbb C$, and $f_j\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb M;\\mathbb C)$. For a finite integer $s_j\\ge0$, the $s_j$-jet is $j^{s_j}\\phi(x)=(\\partial^\\alpha\\phi(x))_{\\alpha\\in\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}^4,\\,|\\alpha|\\le s_j}$; each $P_j$ is a complex polynomial in these finitely many jet coordinates. This is the local functional class used by the supplied scalar time-ordered system.\n\nFor every mass label $m\\ge0$, suppose a normalized scalar time-ordered system $T_n^{(m)}$ with the same local scalar contract is supplied. Define its coefficientwise formal S-map by\n$$\\mathcal S_m(V)=\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{1}{n!}T_n^{(m)}(V,\\ldots,V),\\qquad T_0^{(m)}=1,\\quad T_1^{(m)}=\\operatorname{id}.$$\nThe series is formal in the interaction degree; no analytic convergence is asserted.\n\nUse units $c=\\hbar=1$, so the mass label $m$ has mass dimension one. The scale parameter $\\rho>0$ is dimensionless. On scalar field configurations put\n$$(C_\\rho\\phi)(x)=\\rho^{-1}\\phi(\\rho^{-1}x),$$\nthe engineering scaling of a four-dimensional scalar field. For a mass-indexed family of functionals $F=(F_m)_{m\\ge0}$, define\n$$(\\sigma_\\rho F)_m=C_\\rho^*F_{\\rho^{-1}m},\\qquad (C_\\rho^*F)(\\phi)=F(C_{\\rho^{-1}}\\phi).$$\nThus the component at mass $m$ is pulled back from the component at mass $\\rho^{-1}m$; equivalently the mass label scales as $m\\mapsto\\rho m$. If $A=(A_m)_{m\\ge0}$ is instead a family of maps from local functionals (or their formal series) to local or microcausal functionals (or their formal series), define its induced scale conjugate, denoted $\\sigma_\\rho A\\sigma_\\rho^{-1}$, by\n$$(\\sigma_\\rho A\\sigma_\\rho^{-1})_m=C_\\rho^*\\circ A_{\\rho^{-1}m}\\circ(C_\\rho^*)^{-1},\\qquad (C_\\rho^*)^{-1}=C_{\\rho^{-1}}^*.$$\nHere the rightmost pullback acts on the input, the leftmost pullback acts on every output coefficient, and formal indeterminates are fixed. In particular the mass-$m$ component of the scale-conjugated formal S-map is\n$$ (\\sigma_\\rho\\mathcal S\\sigma_\\rho^{-1})_m=C_\\rho^*\\circ\\mathcal S_{\\rho^{-1}m}\\circ(C_\\rho^*)^{-1}.$$\nThese functional and map actions are extended coefficientwise to formal series. They obey $\\sigma_1=\\operatorname{id}$ and $\\sigma_{\\rho\\tau}=\\sigma_\\rho\\sigma_\\tau$; the dilation sends compact supports to compact supports and normalizes the proper orthochronous Poincare action.\n\nFor $m\\ge0$, $\\rho>0$ and each $n\\ge0$, define the conjugated sequence of multilinear maps componentwise by\n$$T'^{(m)}_{n,\\rho}(F_1,\\ldots,F_n):=C_\\rho^*\\!\\left[T_n^{(\\rho^{-1}m)}\\bigl((C_\\rho^*)^{-1}F_1,\\ldots,(C_\\rho^*)^{-1}F_n\\bigr)\\right].$$\nFor $n=0$ this means $T'^{(m)}_{0,\\rho}=C_\\rho^*1=1$; for $n=1$ it gives $T'^{(m)}_{1,\\rho}=\\operatorname{id}$. Assume for every $m\\ge0$ and $\\rho>0$ that this sequence, as well as the supplied sequence $(T_n^{(m)})_{n\\ge0}$, satisfies the normalized local scalar time-ordered-system contract for the same free data at mass $m$, after transporting the free data at mass $\\rho^{-1}m$ by the stated pullback. This is the scale-admissibility condition on the sequences. Their formal S-maps are distinct objects: by multilinearity and coefficientwise pullback, the S-map of $(T'^{(m)}_{n,\\rho})_{n\\ge0}$ is exactly $(\\sigma_\\rho\\mathcal S\\sigma_\\rho^{-1})_m$ displayed above. Define $Z_\\rho=(Z_{\\rho,m})_{m\\ge0}$ by the componentwise equations\n$$C_\\rho^*\\circ\\mathcal S_{\\rho^{-1}m}\\circ(C_\\rho^*)^{-1}=\\mathcal S_m\\circ Z_{\\rho,m}\\qquad(m\\ge0),$$\nwhere each $Z_{\\rho,m}$ is the unique formal local map with identity linear term, supplied by the finite-renormalization comparison proposition under these hypotheses. The notation $Z_{\\rho,m}(V)=V+O(V^2)$ means that all terms after the linear term have interaction degree at least two. This is a conditional definition: no existence of a time-ordered system for any particular model is asserted.\n\n","deps":["def-qftx-local-time-ordered-system","prop-qftx-finite-renormalization-composition","def-countable-choice"],"justified_by":[],"forward_refs":[],"dependency_roles":{"def-qftx-local-time-ordered-system":"mathematical-premise","prop-qftx-finite-renormalization-composition":"mathematical-premise","def-countable-choice":"mathematical-premise"},"provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Klaus Fredenhagen and Katarzyna Rejzner, Perturbative algebraic quantum field theory (full lecture notes)","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/1208.1428","locator":"Section 7, equations (79)–(80), coefficientwise formal S-matrices and finite local renormalization maps."},{"title":"Romeo Brunetti, Michael Dütsch, and Klaus Fredenhagen, Perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory and the Renormalization Groups","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/0901.2038","locator":"Section 4.2, equations (60)–(65), scaling action on scalar fields and mass-indexed algebras, and the formal cocycle convention."}]},"physical_scope":null,"dependency_level":10,"strategy":"direct","reference":"items/def-qftx-scaling-cocycle.md#Definition","design_state":"Authored conditional scale-action construction; finite-renormalization supplier readiness remains escalated.","inventory_expansion_required":false},{"id":"prop-qftx-scale-cocycle-law","kind":"proposition","domain":"mathematics","library":"mathematics","title":"Scaling yields the twisted composition law","statement":"Assume Countable Choice.\n\nLet $Z_\\rho$ be the mass-indexed formal renormalization map defined for the scale action $\\sigma_\\rho$, $\\rho>0$. For all $\\rho,\\tau>0$,\n$$Z_{\\rho\\tau}=Z_\\rho\\circ\\bigl(\\sigma_\\rho Z_\\tau\\sigma_\\rho^{-1}\\bigr).$$\nIf $\\sigma_\\rho Z_\\tau\\sigma_\\rho^{-1}=Z_\\tau$ for every $\\rho,\\tau>0$, this reduces to $Z_{\\rho\\tau}=Z_\\rho\\circ Z_\\tau$. No one-parameter subgroup law is asserted without this condition.","deps":["def-qftx-scaling-cocycle","def-countable-choice"],"justified_by":[],"forward_refs":[],"dependency_roles":{"def-qftx-scaling-cocycle":"mathematical-premise","def-countable-choice":"mathematical-premise"},"provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Klaus Fredenhagen and Katarzyna Rejzner, Perturbative algebraic quantum field theory (full lecture notes)","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/1208.1428","locator":"Section 7, equations (79)–(80), coefficientwise formal S-matrices and unique finite local renormalization maps."},{"title":"Romeo Brunetti, Michael Dütsch, and Klaus Fredenhagen, Perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory and the Renormalization Groups","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/0901.2038","locator":"Section 4.2, equations (60)–(65), the scale automorphism and twisted composition law; Section 5.1, equations (68)–(70), mass-dependent conjugation."}]},"physical_scope":null,"dependency_level":11,"strategy":"direct","reference":"items/prop-qftx-scale-cocycle-law.md#Statement","design_state":"Authored direct cocycle derivation; its definition supplier remains conditional on unresolved finite-renormalization readiness.","inventory_expansion_required":false},{"id":"thm-qftx-truncated-rg-ode","kind":"theorem","domain":"mathematics","library":"mathematics","title":"One-coupling truncated RG flow and leading beta-coefficient invariance","statement":"Let $\\rho>0$ be a dimensionless scale and put $t=\\log\\rho\\in\\mathbb R$. Take $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ to be an interval containing $0$. Let $g:I\\to\\mathbb R$ be a differentiable real coupling and let $b,g_0\\in\\mathbb R$ be dimensionless constants; require $g$ to satisfy the truncated scalar initial-value problem\n$$\\frac{dg}{dt}=bg^2,\\qquad g(0)=g_0.$$\nA solution through $(0,g_0)$ is **maximal** if there is no strictly larger interval $J\\supsetneq I$ and solution $G:J\\to\\mathbb R$ of the same initial-value problem with $G|_I=g$. If $c:=bg_0=0$, the unique maximal real solution is $g(t)=g_0$ on $\\mathbb R$. If $c\\ne0$, the unique maximal solution through $t=0$ is\n$$g(t)=\\frac{g_0}{1-bg_0t}$$\non $(-\\infty,c^{-1})$ when $c>0$ and on $(c^{-1},\\infty)$ when $c<0$.\n\nFor the formal beta-series calculation, let $u$ be an indeterminate and work in $\\mathbb R[ [u] ]$. Write $\\beta(u)=bu^2+O(u^3)$ and let $\\Phi(u)=u+au^2+O(u^3)$ be an invertible formal change of dimensionless coupling, with dimensionless $a\\in\\mathbb R$. The notation $O(u^k)$ means an element of $u^k\\mathbb R[ [u] ]$, so its terms have degree at least $k$; the real trajectory $g(t)$ is distinct from the formal indeterminate $u$. Put $\\widetilde u=\\Phi(u)$, let $\\Phi'$ be the termwise formal derivative, and define the transformed formal vector field in $\\mathbb R[ [\\widetilde u] ]$ by\n$$\\widetilde\\beta(\\widetilde u)=\\bigl(\\Phi'(u)\\beta(u)\\bigr)\\big|_{u=\\Phi^{-1}(\\widetilde u)}.$$\nThe quadratic coefficient of $\\widetilde\\beta$ is $b$. The real ODE is exact only for the displayed truncation; neither it nor the formal scheme-change statement determines $b$ from a QFT graph or bounds omitted higher-order terms. Its pole is a property of this truncated equation, not a theorem about an ultraviolet completion.\n\n","deps":["def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution","def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable","thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness"],"justified_by":[],"forward_refs":[],"dependency_roles":{"def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution":"mathematical-premise","def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable":"mathematical-premise","thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness":"mathematical-premise"},"provenance":{"statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated"},"sources":{"references":[{"title":"Romeo Brunetti, Michael Dütsch, and Klaus Fredenhagen, Perturbative Algebraic Quantum Field Theory and the Renormalization Groups","url":"https://arxiv.org/html/0901.2038","locator":"Sections 5.1–5.2 and 7.2, mass-dependent cocycle, beta function, and the distinction between formal scale structure and a model-specific coefficient calculation."},{"title":"Renormalization Scheme Dependence, University of Texas at Austin QFT II lecture notes","url":"https://web2.ph.utexas.edu/~vadim/Classes/2022f/ms.pdf","locator":"Printed pages 1–2, equations (2)–(9), beta-series transformation under a finite coupling redefinition; the local proof uses only the leading coefficient."}]},"physical_scope":null,"dependency_level":0,"strategy":"direct","reference":"items/thm-qftx-truncated-rg-ode.md#Statement","design_state":"Authored conditional model calculation with explicit ODE uniqueness inputs and no physical beta coefficient claim.","inventory_expansion_required":false}],"scaffold":"research/plan-quantum-field-theory-frontier-expansion-track.md","order":827}
