+---
+id: def-aqft-scheme-and-wilson-coefficients
+kind: definition
+title: Finite renormalization schemes and Wilson coefficient vectors
+status: draft
+origin: pipeline
+domain: mathematics
+library: mathematics
+physical_scope: null
+dependency_level: 3
+deps:
+  - def-aqft-local-operator-and-dimension
+  - def-aqft-operator-basis-quotient
+justified_by: []
+forward_refs: []
+dependency_roles:
+  def-aqft-local-operator-and-dimension: mathematical-premise
+  def-aqft-operator-basis-quotient: mathematical-premise
+provenance:
+  statement: ai-altered
+  proof: not-applicable
+sources:
+  references:
+    - title: Aneesh V. Manohar, Introduction to Effective Field Theories (2018 Les Houches full lecture notes)
+      url: https://arxiv.org/pdf/1804.05863
+      locator: Chapter 2, §2 Renormalization, equations (6)–(11) and scale-dependence discussion; Chapter 4, §10 RG Improved Perturbation Theory, equations (49)–(52).
+    - title: C. P. Burgess, Introduction to Effective Field Theory (full review)
+      url: https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0701053
+      locator: §§2.2.1–2.2.2 on the Wilson scale and renormalization; §4.1.3, equations (73)–(79), for physical and MS scheme conventions and running.
+verification:
+  precheck: n/a
+---
+
+## Definition
+
+Work in four spacetime dimensions and natural units $c=\hbar=1$. Let $V$ be a
+supplied finite-dimensional complex quotient of local operator classes at
+fixed field content, symmetry data, and finite engineering-dimension order.
+Let $N=\dim_{\mathbb C}V\ge1$, choose an ordered basis
+$O_1,\ldots,O_N$ of $V$, and let $\Delta_i$ be the declared engineering
+dimension of $O_i$. These quotient and dimension data are inputs; their
+construction is not asserted here. For a fixed cutoff $\Lambda>0$ of mass
+dimension one, coordinate $C_i(\mu)$ is normalized so that its formal summand
+is $C_i(\mu)O_i/\Lambda^{\Delta_i-4}$. Each $C_i(\mu)\in\mathbb C$ is
+dimensionless and each summand has mass dimension four. The sum is a formal
+coefficient expression in the supplied quotient, not a constructed action.
+No Hermiticity or reality condition is imposed; a real-action condition is
+additional data.
+
+A finite renormalization-scheme record consists of a nonempty set $B$ of
+input labels, an open interval $J\subset(0,\infty)$ of renormalization scales,
+and a family of coefficient-coordinate maps
+$$\mathcal R_\mu:B\longrightarrow\mathbb R^N\qquad(\mu\in J).$$
+The maps include the chosen subtraction and normalization convention; they
+are supplied data, not constructed in this definition. For $b\in B$, the
+scale-dependent Wilson coefficient vector is
+$$C_{\mathcal R}(\mu;b):=\mathcal R_\mu(b).$$
+The renormalization scale $\mu$ and the cutoff $\Lambda$ are distinct inputs.
+Choose a reference scale $\mu_0\in J$ and set
+$$t:=\log(\mu/\mu_0),\qquad I:=\{\log(\mu/\mu_0):\mu\in J\};$$
+then $t$ is dimensionless and $I$ is an open interval containing $0$.
+
+Define the realification $\rho_N:\mathbb C^N\to\mathbb R^{2N}$ by
+$$\rho_N(C_1,\ldots,C_N)=(\operatorname{Re}C_1,\operatorname{Im}C_1,\ldots,\operatorname{Re}C_N,\operatorname{Im}C_N).$$
+A supplied finite-dimensional truncated RG datum for this scheme is an open
+set $D\subset I\times\mathbb R^{2N}$ and a supplied continuous map
+$\beta:D\to\mathbb R^{2N}$. A truncated RG trajectory is a continuously
+differentiable curve $C:I\to\mathbb C^N$ for which
+$$\frac{d}{dt}\rho_N(C(t))=\beta(t,\rho_N(C(t))).$$
+The components of $\beta$ are the beta functions under this logarithmic-scale
+convention. If the flow is complex-linear in $C$, it may instead be written
+$dC/dt=\Gamma(t)C$ for a supplied $N\times N$ complex matrix $\Gamma(t)$;
+its real-coordinate matrix is $\rho_N\Gamma(t)\rho_N^{-1}$. The matrix
+$\Gamma$ is the anomalous-dimension matrix in the column-vector convention
+fixed here. A scheme record does not assert that its coefficient-coordinate
+maps solve a supplied RG equation; that relation, the entries of $\beta$,
+and any matching map are separate inputs. No QFT beta function or higher-order
+error bound is calculated by this definition.
+
+## Definition references
+
+[[def-aqft-local-operator-and-dimension]]
+
+[[def-aqft-operator-basis-quotient]]
+
+[Manohar, full EFT lecture notes](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1804.05863)
+
+[Burgess, full EFT review](https://arxiv.org/pdf/hep-th/0701053)
+
+## Remarks
+
+The definition is conditional on the supplied finite operator quotient and its
+engineering-dimension data. The current batch-16 item carriers are present and
+provide a complex quotient by an explicitly specified invariant relation
+subspace; they are not yet listed in the batch-16 page manifest. This
+definition uses only that quotient interface and does not claim that its
+relation space captures every physical integration-by-parts, equation-of-motion,
+or field-redefinition equivalence. The formal coefficient maps and beta vector
+field are not claimed to arise from a particular regulator, path integral, or
+QFT.
