+---
+id: thm-aqft-truncated-coupled-rge-wellposedness
+kind: theorem
+title: Local well-posedness of a supplied truncated coupled RG system
+status: draft
+origin: pipeline
+domain: mathematics
+library: mathematics
+physical_scope: null
+dependency_level: 4
+deps:
+  - def-aqft-scheme-and-wilson-coefficients
+  - def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution
+  - def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable
+  - prop-first-order-ivp-is-equivalent-to-a-volterra-integral-equation
+  - thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness
+justified_by: []
+forward_refs: []
+dependency_roles:
+  def-aqft-scheme-and-wilson-coefficients: mathematical-premise
+  def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution: mathematical-premise
+  def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable: mathematical-premise
+  prop-first-order-ivp-is-equivalent-to-a-volterra-integral-equation: mathematical-premise
+  thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness: mathematical-premise
+proof_strategy: direct
+provenance:
+  statement: ai-altered
+  proof: ai-generated
+sources:
+  references:
+    - title: Gerald Teschl, Ordinary Differential Equations and Dynamical Systems (author-hosted complete PDF)
+      url: https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-ode/ode.pdf
+      locator: Chapter 2 §2.2, equations (2.10)–(2.20), Theorem 2.2 and its complete proof; compact-cylinder bounds and local Picard contraction.
+    - title: A. Bouin, Cours Differential Equations, 2018–2019 (full lecture notes)
+      url: https://www.ceremade.dauphine.fr/~bouin/ens1819/EquaDiff_CoursL3_2018-2019.pdf
+      locator: §1.5, Theorem 1.5.4 and Lemma 1.5.5, PDF pp. 12–15; local contraction, first-exit uniqueness, and uniqueness on the common interval.
+verification:
+  precheck: pending
+---
+
+## Statement
+
+Let a scheme datum have complex coefficient dimension $N\ge1$. In its
+realification, let the open RG domain be $D\subseteq\mathbb R\times\mathbb R^{2N}$
+and the supplied vector field be $\beta:D\to\mathbb R^{2N}$,
+continuous and locally Lipschitz in the coefficient coordinates, locally
+uniformly in the logarithmic scale. For every $(t_0,x_0)\in D$, there are
+$r,h>0$ and a finite constant $L\ge0$ such that, for every
+$y,z\in B(x_0,r)$, the initial value problems
+$$x_y'(t)=\beta(t,x_y(t)),\qquad x_y(t_0)=y,$$
+and
+$$x_z'(t)=\beta(t,x_z(t)),\qquad x_z(t_0)=z,$$
+have unique solutions on $[t_0-h,t_0+h]$, with their graphs in $D$, and
+$$\|x_y(t)-x_z(t)\|_2\le e^{L|t-t_0|}\|y-z\|_2\qquad (t\in[t_0-h,t_0+h]).$$
+Any two solutions through the same initial data agree on every common
+interval containing $t_0$.
+Here local well-posedness means local existence, uniqueness, and this uniform
+continuous dependence on initial coefficient data. Under $C=\rho_N^{-1}(x)$,
+the realified curves give the complex coefficient trajectories. The theorem asserts no
+global solution, no bound on omitted perturbative or higher-dimension terms,
+and no calculation of a QFT beta function.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[[def-aqft-scheme-and-wilson-coefficients]]
+
+[[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]
+
+[[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]
+
+[[thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness]]
+
+[[prop-first-order-ivp-is-equivalent-to-a-volterra-integral-equation]]
+
+[Teschl, full author-hosted ODE book](https://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~gerald/ftp/book-ode/ode.pdf)
+
+[Bouin, full differential-equations lecture notes](https://www.ceremade.dauphine.fr/~bouin/ens1819/EquaDiff_CoursL3_2018-2019.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** The scheme datum, $(t_0,x_0)\in D$, and the continuity and local state-Lipschitz hypotheses in the Statement.
+
+[F1] [[def-aqft-scheme-and-wilson-coefficients]]: the coefficient space is $\mathbb C^N$ with realification $\rho_N(C)=(\operatorname{Re}C_1,\operatorname{Im}C_1,\ldots,\operatorname{Re}C_N,\operatorname{Im}C_N)\in\mathbb R^{2N}$; the supplied RG domain is open in $I\times\mathbb R^{2N}$ and $\beta$ maps it to $\mathbb R^{2N}$.
+
+[F2] [[def-first-order-ode-initial-value-problem-and-solution]]: a solution is a differentiable curve in the open domain, through the initial value, whose derivative equals the vector field; a local solution is defined on an interval containing a nondegenerate neighborhood of the initial time.
+
+[F3] [[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]: on every compact time-state cylinder in the domain, one finite constant $L$ bounds $\|\beta(t,x)-\beta(t,y)\|_2/\|x-y\|_2$ for pairs with the same time coordinate.
+
+[F4] [[thm-picard-lindelof-local-existence-and-uniqueness]]: for a continuous vector field on an open finite-dimensional domain that is locally Lipschitz in the state variable uniformly in time, a unique local solution exists, and any two solutions with the same initial data agree on every common interval containing that time. On a compact cylinder where $\|\beta\|_2\le M$ and the Lipschitz constant is $L$, any $h,r>0$ with $hM\le r$ and $Lh<1$ give a unique solution on the corresponding short interval whose graph stays in the cylinder.
+
+[F5] [[prop-first-order-ivp-is-equivalent-to-a-volterra-integral-equation]]: a continuous curve solves the IVP exactly when it satisfies $C(t)=C(t_0)+\int_{t_0}^{t}\beta(s,C(s))\,ds$, with the integral oriented on either side of $t_0$.
+
+## Proof
+
+**Proof technique:** apply the quantitative Picard–Lindelöf theorem on one compact cylinder, then prove the dependence estimate by iterating its integral inequality.
+
+**Given:** [F1]–[F5].
+
+1.1 Since $D$ is open and $(t_0,x_0)\in D$, choose $r,h_0>0$ so the compact cylinder $K=[t_0-h_0,t_0+h_0]\times\overline B(x_0,2r)$ lies in $D$. Continuity of $\beta$ on $K$ gives a finite bound $M$, and [F3] gives a single state-Lipschitz constant $L$ there. Choose $0<h\le h_0$ with $h(M+1)\le r$ and $h(L+1)<1$; then $hM\le r$ and $Lh<1$. [given, F1, F3, construct]
+
+2.1 For every $y\in B(x_0,r)$, the cylinder $[t_0-h,t_0+h]\times\overline B(y,r)$ lies in $K$. Its vector-field and Lipschitz bounds are at most $M$ and $L$. Applying the quantitative clause of [F4] with $hM\le r$ and $Lh<1$ gives a unique solution $x_y$ on this same interval, with graph in that cylinder. The common-interval uniqueness clause of [F4] also gives agreement on every larger common interval containing the initial time. In particular, taking $y=x_0$ gives the asserted local solution through $x_0$. [given, F4, step 1.1, algebra]
+
+3.1 Take $y,z\in B(x_0,r)$ and put $a=\|y-z\|_2$. By [F5], the difference of their solution curves is the initial difference plus the oriented integral of the beta difference. Since both graphs lie in $K$, [F3] and the triangle inequality for the componentwise integral give, for $u(t)=\|x_y(t)-x_z(t)\|_2$,
+$$u(t)\le a+L\int_{\min(t,t_0)}^{\max(t,t_0)}u(s)\,ds.$$
+Moreover $u(t)\le4r$: each curve stays within radius $r$ of its own initial value, and $y,z$ are each within radius $r$ of $C_0$. [F3, F5, step 2.1, algebra]
+
+4.1 If $t\ne t_0$, set $d=|t-t_0|$, $\varepsilon=\operatorname{sgn}(t-t_0)$, and $v(q)=u(t_0+\varepsilon q)$ for $0\le q\le d$. Step 3.1 gives $v(q)\le a+L\int_0^qv(s)\,ds$ and $v\le U:=4r$. Repeated substitution, by induction on $m\ge1$, yields
+$$v(d)\le a\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}\frac{(Ld)^j}{j!}+U\frac{(Ld)^m}{m!}.$$
+The base case follows from $v\le U$ in the integral remainder; the induction step integrates each power $s^j$ and uses $\int_0^d s^j\,ds=d^{j+1}/(j+1)$. [step 3.1, algebra]
+
+5.1 For fixed $Ld$, the remainder $(Ld)^m/m!$ tends to zero since the ratio of successive terms is $Ld/(m+1)\to0$, while the partial sums converge to $e^{Ld}$. Letting $m\to\infty$ in step 4.1 gives $u(t)\le a e^{L|t-t_0|}$. At $t=t_0$ the same estimate is equality. This proves the stated continuous-dependence bound and completes the local well-posedness claim. [step 4.1, algebra] ∎
+
+## Remarks
+
+The proof establishes a local statement for the supplied vector field. The
+finite operator quotient is taken at the current batch-16 definition interface;
+no assertion is made that its relation space is physically exhaustive. No
+perturbative coefficient, matching relation, global flow, or error estimate
+follows from this ODE theorem.
