## Example

Assume the full Axiom of Choice and take a supplied finite scheme datum whose
real operator quotient has dimension two, with ordered basis $O_1,O_2$ of
common engineering dimension six and cutoff $\Lambda>0$. In natural units use
the dimensionless coefficient vector $C(t)=(C_1(t),C_2(t))^T$ and write its
formal coefficient expression in the quotient as
$$\mathcal L_{\mathrm{trunc}}(t)=\frac{C_1(t)O_1+C_2(t)O_2}{\Lambda^2},\qquad t=\log(\mu/\mu_0).$$
Supply the constant coefficient-flow matrix
$$A=\begin{pmatrix}-1&1\\0&-2\end{pmatrix}$$
and the initial vector $C(0)=(1,1)^T$. The resulting truncated system is
$C'(t)=AC(t)$, and its solution on all $t\in\mathbb R$ is
$$C(t)=\begin{pmatrix}2e^{-t}-e^{-2t}\\e^{-2t}\end{pmatrix}.$$
The matrix is supplied as part of this mathematical example; it is not
calculated from a QFT and gives no estimate for omitted terms.

## Example references

[[def-axiom-of-choice]]

[[def-aqft-scheme-and-wilson-coefficients]]

[[thm-aqft-truncated-coupled-rge-wellposedness]]

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** A finite scheme datum with the two-dimensional coefficient basis in the Example, the displayed matrix $A$, and initial value $C(0)=(1,1)^T$.

[A1] Assume full AC [[def-axiom-of-choice]] as required by the scheme's conditional operator-quotient input.

[F1] [[def-aqft-scheme-and-wilson-coefficients]]: in the specified basis, the coefficient vector is a real column vector and $t=\log(\mu/\mu_0)$ is dimensionless.

[F2] [[thm-aqft-truncated-coupled-rge-wellposedness]]: a supplied continuous vector field that is locally Lipschitz in the coefficient state has unique local solutions, unique agreement on common intervals, and the local continuous-dependence conclusion stated there.

## Verification

**Proof technique:** direct differentiation and the local well-posedness theorem.

**Given:** [A1], [F1], [F2], and the data in the Example.

1.1 At $t=0$, the displayed vector has components $2-1=1$ and $e^0=1$, so it satisfies the initial condition. [given, algebra]

2.1 Differentiating gives $C_1'(t)=-2e^{-t}+2e^{-2t}$ and $C_2'(t)=-2e^{-2t}$. The first component of $AC$ is $-C_1+C_2=-2e^{-t}+2e^{-2t}$, and the second is $-2C_2=-2e^{-2t}$. Thus the displayed curve solves $C'=AC$ for every real $t$. [given, step 1.1, algebra]

3.1 The vector field $\beta(C)=AC$ is continuous. For $\Delta=C-D$, its first component satisfies $|{-}\Delta_1+\Delta_2|\le\sqrt2\|\Delta\|_2$ and its second satisfies $|{-}2\Delta_2|\le2\|\Delta\|_2$, so $\|A\Delta\|_2^2\le6\|\Delta\|_2^2$. Thus $\beta$ is globally Lipschitz. By [F2], solutions with the same initial value agree on every common interval. The displayed solution exists on all $\mathbb R$, so it is the unique global solution for this supplied system. [A1, F2, step 1.1, step 2.1, algebra] ∎

